---
// Lesson 10.1a — 2-Button Jam: Kickoff & Pitch
// rpg jam: the constraint jam. Pitch, scope, 2 inputs only.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1a — 2-Button Jam: Kickoff &amp; Pitch</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Jam season. First up: a constraint jam — your ENTIRE game reads
    exactly two inputs. Not two keys, TWO INPUTS, whatever they mean.
    Constraints breed creativity because they steal your excuses:
    with everything allowed, you start with nothing; with almost
    nothing allowed, you start with a puzzle.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Kickoff</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What counts as an input?</strong> Anything that
    becomes a signal: two keys, two buttons, one key + one mouse
    click, a touch's left half vs right half, keyboard-only arrow
    keys COUNT AS FOUR (banned!). Past-hit examples: one-button
    runners (flap = jump), one-button bosses (hold longer = higher
    jump), two-button fighters (left dodge, right attack). Every
    input you OWN, you must OWN COMPLETELY: timing, meaning in
    context, held vs. tapped.</p>

    <p><strong>Scoping rules for the 6-day jam (build days are
    <a href="10.1b.html">10.1b</a>–<a href="10.1e.html">10.1e</a>):</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>ONE verb is a mechanic; TWO verbs is a game; THREE is a
            scope lie. You have two buttons. Your game has two
            verbs. Done.</li>
        <li>Reuse what you own: any starter code, your palette
            class (<a href="9.2.html">9.2</a>), your jump feel
            (<a href="6.3.html">6.3</a>), your cooldown pattern.
            A jam is not a clean-slate test — it is a fluency
            test.</li>
        <li>The pitch sells the IDEA in one sentence: "It's ___
            where the only two buttons are ___ and ___."</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The starter's PlayerController reads: WASD, E, Space,
            mouse click — about eight inputs. Name TWO you would
            keep for a two-button game and what each MEANS when it
            is your only verb. Why does fewer inputs make each
            input's FEEL more important (think:
            <a href="6.3.html">6.3</a>'s mercies, but for
            buttons)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pitch &amp; Vote</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Write your pitch card (10 min):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>One sentence: the game ("a cave where you can only DUCK
            and DASH")</li>
        <li>Two verbs and their feel words (duck = commitment, dash
            = panic)</li>
        <li>One image sketch: the screen at the most interesting
            MOMENT</li>
        <li>Reused parts list (what you borrow vs. build — the
            jam-ethics version of <a href="9.10.html">9.10</a>
            credits)</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Pitch (30 seconds each) — then team up by vote.</strong>
    Every pitch gets three public votes. Top pitches recruit; you
    may join any team that will have you. Rules: teams of 1–3, every
    member writes code (no idea-guy-only roles), and every game
    ships SOMETHING playable by <a href="10.1d.html">10.1d</a> —
    a finished small game beats an ambitious folder.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Tonight's homework</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Create the jam repo/folder + a jam README (pitch
                card pasted in).</li>
            <li>Write the INPUT table: button, context, meaning,
                feel — your whole spec is this table.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Pitch a two-verb game in one sentence with feel words</li>
            <li>Justify scope: two inputs, one core loop, shipped by 10.1d</li>
            <li>List reused code honestly and your new code honestly</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — the jam rules</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — input reading patterns</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1b.html">10.1b — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 1</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1a" title="10.1a 2-Button Jam: Kickoff &amp; Pitch — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
